{
    "description":  "Labeled merchant/amount/type triples for the categorization eval. Sent straight to POST /api/categorize on categorization-service, which runs the same CategorizationDecider the Kafka listener runs. Extraction is deliberately not in the loop: scoring categorization through the pipeline would make a wrong merchant indistinguishable from a wrong category.",
    "labellingNote":  "Two rules decide the person-to-person cases, and they are a product decision rather than a fact about the data. A CREDIT from an individual is Transfers, not Income - money arriving from a person is not earnings. A DEBIT to a bare personal name with no further context is \u0027Needs Manual Categorization\u0027, because rent, a loan repayment and a split dinner bill are indistinguishable from the merchant string alone and only the account holder knows which it was. Money between the holder\u0027s own accounts is Transfers in either direction. The categorization prompt previously said to use Transfers whenever money was \u0027sent to/from a known individual\u0027, which contradicted the second rule; it now states the direction rule explicitly, so the gap the eval used to report is closed and these labels grade against the prompt rather than against the product\u0027s intent alone. The pair direction-debit-meera / direction-credit-meera exists to measure exactly that: same name, same amount, opposite direction, different correct answer.",
